# Migration Challenge: Implementing Factory Method with Dependency Injection in Classic Design Patterns

## Overview

In this document, we will explore how to implement a Factory Method pattern with Dependency Injection (DI) in a legacy online payment processing system. Our goal is to refactor the existing tightly coupled codebase to improve flexibility, maintainability, and testability, all while ensuring high performance and compliance with industry standards. 

## 1. Complete Implementations

### Java (Spring Boot)

```java
// Payment.java - Interface for payment processing
public interface Payment {
    void processPayment(double amount);
}

// CreditCardPayment.java - Concrete implementation for credit card payments
public class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {
    @Override
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Processing credit card payment of $" + amount);
        // Payment processing logic...
    }
}

// PayPalPayment.java - Concrete implementation for PayPal payments
public class PayPalPayment implements Payment {
    @Override
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Processing PayPal payment of $" + amount);
        // Payment processing logic...
    }
}

// PaymentFactory.java - Factory class to create payment instances
public class PaymentFactory {
    public static Payment createPayment(String type) {
        if ("CREDIT_CARD".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
            return new CreditCardPayment();
        } else if ("PAYPAL".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
            return new PayPalPayment();
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown payment type: " + type);
    }
}

// PaymentService.java - Service class using the factory
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class PaymentService {
    public void makePayment(String type, double amount) {
        Payment payment = PaymentFactory.createPayment(type);
        payment.processPayment(amount);
    }
}

// Application.java - Main class to run the Spring Boot application
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

### Python (FastAPI)

