In this implementation guide, I will detail the Observer Pattern for an event-driven notification system suitable for a large-scale e-commerce platform. This design will ensure real-time updates across various components while maintaining high performance and scalability.

### Overview of the Observer Pattern

The Observer Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that defines a one-to-many relationship between objects. When one object (the subject) changes its state, all registered observers are notified and updated automatically. This pattern is particularly useful in event-driven systems, as it promotes loose coupling between components.

### Event Types

We will define the following three events:

1. `OrderPlaced`
2. `InventoryUpdated`
3. `UserRegistered`

### Observer Components

We will implement five observer components:

1. **Inventory Service**
2. **Email Notification Service**
3. **Analytics Service**
4. **User Dashboard Service**
5. **Accounting Service**

### Implementation in Java

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Event Interface
interface Event {}

// Event Types
class OrderPlaced implements Event {
    private final String orderId;

    public OrderPlaced(String orderId) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
    }

    public String getOrderId() {
        return orderId;
    }
}

class InventoryUpdated implements Event {
    private final String productId;
    private final int newStockLevel;

    public InventoryUpdated(String productId, int newStockLevel) {
        this.productId = productId;
        this.newStockLevel = newStockLevel;
    }

    public String getProductId() {
        return productId;
    }

    public int getNewStockLevel() {
        return newStockLevel;
    }
}

class UserRegistered implements Event {
    private final String userId;

    public UserRegistered(String userId) {
        this.userId = userId;
    }

    public String getUserId() {
        return userId;
    }
}

// Observer Interface
interface Observer {
    void update(Event event);
}

// Subject Class
class EventManager {
    private final List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();

    public void subscribe(Observer observer) {
        observers.add(observer);
    }

    public void unsubscribe(Observer observer) {
        observers.remove(observer);
    }

    public void notify(Event event) {
        for (Observer observer : observers) {
            observer.update(event);
        }
    }
}

// Concrete Observers
class InventoryService implements Observer {
    @Override
    public void update(Event event) {
        if (event instanceof OrderPlaced) {
            System.out.println("Inventory Service: Order placed with ID " + ((OrderPlaced) event).getOrderId());
        } else if (event instanceof InventoryUpdated) {
            System.out.println("Inventory Service: Product " + ((InventoryUpdated) event).getProductId() + " stock updated to " + ((InventoryUpdated) event).getNewStockLevel());
        }
    }
}

class EmailNotificationService implements Observer {
    @Override
    public void update(Event event) {
        if (event instanceof OrderPlaced) {
            System.out.println("Email Notification Service: Sending email for order " + ((OrderPlaced) event).getOrderId());
        }
    }
}

// Other services would be implemented similarly...

// Main Application
public class ECommerceNotificationSystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EventManager eventManager = new EventManager();

        InventoryService inventoryService = new InventoryService();
        EmailNotificationService emailService = new EmailNotificationService();

        eventManager.subscribe(inventoryService);
        eventManager.subscribe(emailService);

        // Simulating events
        eventManager.notify(new OrderPlaced("12345"));
        eventManager.notify(new InventoryUpdated("product-1", 50));
    }
}
```

