## Event Loop Implementation for Asynchronous I/O in a Reactive Context

### Overview

An Event Loop is a programming construct that waits for and dispatches events or messages in a program. It is particularly useful in asynchronous I/O operations where it can handle multiple tasks without blocking the thread. In the context of a large-scale e-commerce platform, the Event Loop should efficiently handle a large number of concurrent user requests, while ensuring low latency and high throughput.

### 1. Design the Event Loop

#### Architecture

For our implementation, we will use Java with the **Vert.x** framework, which provides a highly scalable event-driven architecture suited for handling asynchronous I/O.

#### Code Structure

```java
import io.vertx.core.Vertx;
import io.vertx.core.Future;
import io.vertx.core.http.HttpServer;
import io.vertx.ext.web.Router;

public class EventLoopExample {
    private final Vertx vertx = Vertx.vertx();
    private final Router router = Router.router(vertx);

    public EventLoopExample() {
        setupRoutes();
        startServer();
    }

    private void setupRoutes() {
        router.get("/api/data").handler(routingContext -> {
            Future<String> data = fetchDataAsync();
            data.onComplete(ar -> {
                if (ar.succeeded()) {
                    routingContext.response()
                        .setStatusCode(200)
                        .end(ar.result());
                } else {
                    routingContext.response()
                        .setStatusCode(500)
                        .end("Internal Server Error");
                }
            });
        });
    }

    private Future<String> fetchDataAsync() {
        return Future.future(promise -> {
            vertx.executeBlocking(future -> {
                // Simulate I/O operation (e.g., database call)
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100); // Simulating delay
                    future.complete("Fetched Data");
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    future.fail("Failed to fetch data");
                }
            }, promise);
        });
    }

    private void startServer() {
        HttpServer server = vertx.createHttpServer();
        server.requestHandler(router).listen(8080);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new EventLoopExample();
    }
}
```

