# Implementing an Identity Map in a High-Throughput E-Commerce Application

The Identity Map pattern is a design pattern that can significantly enhance the performance of data retrieval in an application by caching objects in memory. This is particularly useful in high-throughput systems, such as e-commerce platforms, where frequent database calls can lead to bottlenecks. In this comprehensive guide, we will implement the Identity Map pattern in a high-throughput e-commerce application, covering various aspects including the structure, concurrency handling, performance metrics, and real-world examples.

## 1. Identity Map Structure

### Java Implementation

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class IdentityMap<T> {
    private final Map<String, T> map = new HashMap<>();

    public T get(String id) {
        return map.get(id);
    }

    public void put(String id, T object) {
        map.put(id, object);
    }

    public void clear() {
        map.clear();
    }
}
```

### Python Implementation

```python
class IdentityMap:
    def __init__(self):
        self.map = {}

    def get(self, id):
        return self.map.get(id)

    def put(self, id, obj):
        self.map[id] = obj

    def clear(self):
        self.map.clear()
```

### Go Implementation

```go
package main

import "sync"

type IdentityMap struct {
    sync.RWMutex
    mapData map[string]interface{}
}

func NewIdentityMap() *IdentityMap {
    return &IdentityMap{
        mapData: make(map[string]interface{}),
    }
}

func (im *IdentityMap) Get(id string) (interface{}, bool) {
    im.RLock()
    defer im.RUnlock()
    obj, exists := im.mapData[id]
    return obj, exists
}

func (im *IdentityMap) Put(id string, obj interface{}) {
    im.Lock()
    defer im.Unlock()
    im.mapData[id] = obj
}

func (im *IdentityMap) Clear() {
    im.Lock()
    defer im.Unlock()
    im.mapData = make(map[string]interface{})
}
```

