## 3. Real-World Examples

### Netflix
Netflix employs lazy loading in their streaming service. When a user browses content, only metadata (e.g., title, description, thumbnail) is initially loaded. When the user selects a movie, additional data (e.g., reviews, recommendations) is loaded on demand.

### Amazon
Amazon uses lazy loading for product detail pages. Initial loads show basic product information, and detailed specifications and reviews load as the user scrolls or interacts with the page, optimizing the experience while managing server load.

### Uber
Uber utilizes lazy loading for its driver and rider applications. Information related to drivers (e.g., ratings, ride history) is not loaded until specifically requested, ensuring faster app responsiveness during peak usage.

### Google
Google's search results page uses lazy loading by displaying initial results quickly and fetching additional data as the user interacts with the page. This reduces the load time and improves user experience on resource-intensive queries.

