# Advanced Implementation of the Specification Pattern in an E-commerce Application

## Introduction

The Specification Pattern is an essential design pattern that allows for encapsulating business rules and criteria in a flexible and reusable manner. In the context of an e-commerce application, this pattern is particularly beneficial for implementing complex filtering logic for product searches. This document will provide a complete implementation of the Specification Pattern across multiple programming languages (Java, Python, Go, and TypeScript) while addressing real-world contexts, performance considerations, anti-patterns, testing strategies, and trade-offs.

## Domain Model

### Entities

1. **Product**
   - `name`: String
   - `category`: String
   - `price`: Double
   - `isAvailable`: Boolean
   - `promotions`: List<String>

2. **User**
   - `preferredCategories`: List<String>
   - `maxPrice`: Double
   - `isPremiumUser`: Boolean

### Business Rules
Users can filter products based on:
- Availability
- Price
- Category
- Premium promotions

## Specification Pattern Design

### Step 1: Define the Specification Interface

We will start by defining a common interface for the specifications.

```java
// Specification.java (Java)
public interface Specification<T> {
    boolean isSatisfiedBy(T item);
}

// Specification.java (Python)
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Specification(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def is_satisfied_by(self, item) -> bool:
        pass

# Specification.go (Go)
type Specification[T any] interface {
    IsSatisfiedBy(item T) bool
}

// Specification.ts (TypeScript)
interface Specification<T> {
    isSatisfiedBy(item: T): boolean;
}
```

### Step 2: Create Concrete Specification Classes

#### Availability Specification

```java
// AvailabilitySpecification.java (Java)
public class AvailabilitySpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    @Override
    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product product) {
        return product.isAvailable();
    }
}

// availability_specification.py (Python)
class AvailabilitySpecification(Specification):
    def is_satisfied_by(self, item) -> bool:
        return item.is_available

// availability_specification.go (Go)
type AvailabilitySpecification struct{}

func (s AvailabilitySpecification) IsSatisfiedBy(item Product) bool {
    return item.IsAvailable
}

// availability_specification.ts (TypeScript)
class AvailabilitySpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    isSatisfiedBy(product: Product): boolean {
        return product.isAvailable;
    }
}
```

#### Price Specification

```java
// PriceSpecification.java (Java)
public class PriceSpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private final double maxPrice;

    public PriceSpecification(double maxPrice) {
        this.maxPrice = maxPrice;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product product) {
        return product.getPrice() <= maxPrice;
    }
}

// price_specification.py (Python)
class PriceSpecification(Specification):
    def __init__(self, max_price):
        self.max_price = max_price

    def is_satisfied_by(self, item) -> bool:
        return item.price <= self.max_price

// price_specification.go (Go)
type PriceSpecification struct {
    MaxPrice float64
}

func (s PriceSpecification) IsSatisfiedBy(item Product) bool {
    return item.Price <= s.MaxPrice
}

// price_specification.ts (TypeScript)
class PriceSpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private maxPrice: number;

    constructor(maxPrice: number) {
        this.maxPrice = maxPrice;
    }

    isSatisfiedBy(product: Product): boolean {
        return product.price <= this.maxPrice;
    }
}
```

#### Category Specification

```java
// CategorySpecification.java (Java)
public class CategorySpecification implements Specification<Product> {
    private final List<String> preferredCategories;

    public CategorySpecification(List<String> preferredCategories) {
        this.preferredCategories = preferredCategories;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isSatisfiedBy(Product product) {
        return preferredCategories.contains(product.getCategory());
    }
}

// category_specification.py (Python)
class CategorySpecification(Specification):
    def __init__(self, preferred_categories):
        self.preferred_categories = preferred_categories

