## 1. Overview of MVVM
The MVVM pattern separates the application into three main components:
- **Model**: Represents the data and business logic. It encapsulates the data structure and validation rules.
- **View**: The UI layer that displays data to users and sends user commands to the ViewModel.
- **ViewModel**: Acts as a bridge between the Model and the View. It exposes data from the Model to the View and handles user input.

## 2. Architecture Design
### Components
- **Frontend**: Angular application for building a dynamic UI.
- **Backend**: Node.js with Express for handling API requests.
- **Database**: PostgreSQL for storing customer and sales data.

### Interaction Flow
1. User interacts with the View.
2. ViewModel receives input and communicates with the Model.
3. Model performs business logic and validation.
4. ViewModel updates the View with data from the Model.

## 3. Complete Code Implementation
### 3.1 Data Models (Node.js with PostgreSQL)
```javascript
// models/customer.js
const { Sequelize, DataTypes } = require('sequelize');
const sequelize = new Sequelize('postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/mydb');

const Customer = sequelize.define('Customer', {
    name: {
        type: DataTypes.STRING,
        allowNull: false
    },
    email: {
        type: DataTypes.STRING,
        allowNull: false,
        validate: {
            isEmail: true
        }
    },
    discount: {
        type: DataTypes.FLOAT,
        defaultValue: 0,
        get() {
            const discountValue = this.getDataValue('discount');
            return discountValue > 0 ? discountValue : 0;
        }
    }
}, {
    hooks: {
        beforeSave: (customer) => {
            if (!customer.email.includes('@')) {
                throw new Error('Email format is invalid');
            }
        }
    }
});

// Export Customer model
module.exports = Customer;
```

### 3.2 View Components (Angular)
```typescript
// src/app/customer/customer.component.ts
import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';
import { CustomerService } from './customer.service';
import { Customer } from './customer.model';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-customer',
  templateUrl: './customer.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./customer.component.css']
})
export class CustomerComponent implements OnInit {
  customers: Customer[] = [];
  loading: boolean = false;

  constructor(private customerService: CustomerService) {}

  ngOnInit() {
    this.loadCustomers();
  }

  loadCustomers() {
    this.loading = true;
    this.customerService.getCustomers().subscribe(data => {
      this.customers = data;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

### 3.3 ViewModels (Angular)
```typescript
// src/app/customer/customer.viewmodel.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { BehaviorSubject, Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { CustomerService } from './customer.service';
import { Customer } from './customer.model';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class CustomerViewModel {
  private customersSubject: BehaviorSubject<Customer[]> = new BehaviorSubject([]);
  public customers$: Observable<Customer[]> = this.customersSubject.asObservable();

  constructor(private customerService: CustomerService) {}

  loadCustomers() {
    this.customerService.getCustomers().subscribe(customers => {
      this.customersSubject.next(customers);
    });
  }

  addCustomer(customer: Customer) {
    this.customerService.addCustomer(customer).subscribe(() => {
      this.loadCustomers();
    });
  }
}
```

