## 3. View Layer

### 3.1 Implementing the View

For this implementation, we'll choose **React** as our modern JavaScript framework.

#### React Implementation

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const PortfolioView = ({ presenter }) => {
    const [investment, setInvestment] = useState(null);
    const [error, setError] = useState(null);

    const handleFetchInvestment = async (id) => {
        try {
            const data = await presenter.getInvestment(id);
            setInvestment(data);
        } catch (err) {
            setError(err.message);
        }
    };

    useEffect(() => {
        handleFetchInvestment("1");
    }, []);

    return (
        <div>
            <h1>Investment Portfolio</h1>
            {error && <p>Error: {error}</p>}
            {investment && (
                <div>
                    <h2>{investment.type} - ${investment.amount}</h2>
                </div>
            )}
        </div>
    );
};

export default PortfolioView;
```

### 3.2 State Management

We manage state within the View using React's hooks (`useState`). The View subscribes to updates from the Presenter, which would fetch data asynchronously from the Model and update the state accordingly. 

