# Composite View Pattern Implementation for E-Commerce Web Application

The Composite View Pattern is a structural design pattern that allows you to compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. This is particularly useful in scenarios where you need to manage a complex user interface that can adapt based on various user roles and contexts, such as in an e-commerce platform. Below, we will explore a complete implementation of this pattern across multiple programming languages, including Java, Python, Go, and TypeScript. 

## 1. Architecture Diagram

![](https://i.imgur.com/5g8bY6Z.png)

### Overview of the Architecture
- **IComponent Interface**: The common interface for all components, both simple and composite.
- **Leaf Components**: Simple UI components like buttons, text fields, etc.
- **Composite Components**: Containers for other components, such as product detail panels.
- **Client Code**: Responsible for creating components and rendering them based on user roles.

## 2. Implementation in Different Languages

### 2.1 Java Implementation

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// IComponent Interface
interface IComponent {
    void render();
    void add(IComponent component);
    void remove(IComponent component);
}

// Leaf Component
class Button implements IComponent {
    private String label;

    public Button(String label) {
        this.label = label;
    }

    @Override
    public void render() {
        System.out.println("Rendering Button: " + label);
    }

    @Override
    public void add(IComponent component) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }

    @Override
    public void remove(IComponent component) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }
}

// Composite Component
class Panel implements IComponent {
    private List<IComponent> components = new ArrayList<>();
    private String title;

    public Panel(String title) {
        this.title = title;
    }

    @Override
    public void render() {
        System.out.println("Rendering Panel: " + title);
        for (IComponent component : components) {
            component.render();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void add(IComponent component) {
        components.add(component);
    }

    @Override
    public void remove(IComponent component) {
        components.remove(component);
    }
}

// Client Code
public class CompositeViewDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IComponent mainPanel = new Panel("Main Panel");
        IComponent button1 = new Button("Submit");
        IComponent button2 = new Button("Cancel");

        mainPanel.add(button1);
        mainPanel.add(button2);

        mainPanel.render();
    }
}
```

### 2.2 Python Implementation

```python
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

# IComponent Interface
class IComponent(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def render(self):
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    def add(self, component):
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    def remove(self, component):
        pass

# Leaf Component
class Button(IComponent):
    def __init__(self, label):
        self.label = label

    def render(self):
        print(f'Rendering Button: {self.label}')

    def add(self, component):
        raise NotImplementedError()

    def remove(self, component):
        raise NotImplementedError()

# Composite Component
class Panel(IComponent):
    def __init__(self, title):
        self.title = title
        self.components = []

    def render(self):
        print(f'Rendering Panel: {self.title}')
        for component in self.components:
            component.render()

    def add(self, component):
        self.components.append(component)

    def remove(self, component):
        self.components.remove(component)

# Client Code
if __name__ == "__main__":
    main_panel = Panel("Main Panel")
    button1 = Button("Submit")
    button2 = Button("Cancel")

    main_panel.add(button1)
    main_panel.add(button2)

    main_panel.render()
```

### 2.3 Go Implementation

```go
package main

import "fmt"

// IComponent Interface
type IComponent interface {
	Render()
	Add(component IComponent)
	Remove(component IComponent)
}

// Leaf Component
type Button struct {
	Label string
}

func (b *Button) Render() {
	fmt.Println("Rendering Button:", b.Label)
}

func (b *Button) Add(component IComponent) {
	panic("Not a composite")
}

func (b *Button) Remove(component IComponent) {
	panic("Not a composite")
}

// Composite Component
type Panel struct {
	Title     string
	Components []IComponent
}

func (p *Panel) Render() {
	fmt.Println("Rendering Panel:", p.Title)
	for _, component := range p.Components {
		component.Render()
	}
}

func (p *Panel) Add(component IComponent) {
	p.Components = append(p.Components, component)
}

func (p *Panel) Remove(component IComponent) {
	for i, comp := range p.Components {
		if comp == component {
			p.Components = append(p.Components[:i], p.Components[i+1:]...)
			break
		}
	}
}

// Client Code
func main() {
	mainPanel := &Panel{Title: "Main Panel"}
	button1 := &Button{Label: "Submit"}
	button2 := &Button{Label: "Cancel"}

	mainPanel.Add(button1)
	mainPanel.Add(button2)

	mainPanel.Render()
}
```

### 2.4 TypeScript Implementation

```typescript
// IComponent Interface
interface IComponent {
    render(): void;
    add(component: IComponent): void;
    remove(component: IComponent): void;
}

// Leaf Component
class Button implements IComponent {
    private label: string;

    constructor(label: string) {
        this.label = label;
    }

    render(): void {
        console.log(`Rendering Button: ${this.label}`);
    }

    add(component: IComponent): void {
        throw new Error("Not a composite");
    }

    remove(component: IComponent): void {
        throw new Error("Not a composite");
    }
}

// Composite Component
class Panel implements IComponent {
    private components: IComponent[] = [];
    private title: string;

    constructor(title: string) {
        this.title = title;
    }

    render(): void {
        console.log(`Rendering Panel: ${this.title}`);
        for (const component of this.components) {
            component.render();
        }
    }

    add(component: IComponent): void {
        this.components.push(component);
    }

    remove(component: IComponent): void {
        this.components = this.components.filter(c => c !== component);
    }
}

// Client Code
const mainPanel = new Panel("Main Panel");
const button1 = new Button("Submit");
const button2 = new Button("Cancel");

mainPanel.add(button1);
mainPanel.add(button2);

mainPanel.render();
```

## 3. Real-World Examples from Tech Companies

### 3.1 Netflix
Netflix employs the Composite View pattern in its UI rendering for different device screens. For example, a single movie detail page can have various components (like trailers, reviews, and recommendations) that are displayed differently based on the device type (TV, mobile, web).

### 3.2 Amazon
Amazon uses a similar pattern for its product detail pages, where different components such as product images, descriptions, and customer reviews are dynamically composed and rendered based on user roles (e.g., Seller vs. Buyer).

### 3.3 Google
Google's admin dashboards utilize the Composite View pattern to manage various settings and configurations. Each component can represent a different setting category and can contain other subcomponents for detailed configurations.

